NON-COMPLIANT (ComplianceViolationAnswer: compliant=false, >=1 violated_meta_rule).

This sidecar is the ANSWER KEY. It is NEVER handed to a judge — only the `.md` candidate is.
The key lived inside an HTML comment in the candidate until 2026-07-29; the sole claim that
it was removed before dispatch lived inside the comment that had to be removed, so a leak
would have been invisible AND would have made the row pass more convincingly.
`.claude/test-harness/tests/probe-suite-integrity.test.mjs` now enforces the separation
mechanically: the candidate may carry no HTML comment and no answer-key marker.

The candidate is a full synthetic rule file, surface-matched to its COMPLIANT counterpart
(`meta-compliant-inference-clause-rule.md`): same frontmatter, same level-2 section skeleton,
same Origin, same Trust-Posture Wiring, sizes within 5%. Every structural affordance the
meta-rules require is PRESENT. The defects are in what those affordances CONTAIN, so a judge
cannot score this pair by asking "which one is a real rule file".

Injected defects, in the order a reader meets them:

  - rule-authoring MUST-1 (modal): the clause heading and body run on "should" / "where
    practical" / "usually optional" / "prefer", and the second sentence RE-PERMITS the exact
    behaviour the clause purports to require ("it is fine to state a well-supported
    conclusion directly"). Detectable from the modal alone.

  - rule-authoring MUST-3 (DO/DO-NOT): the block is PRESENT and well-formed, and its two
    arms are byte-identical in substance — both show the evidence quoted and the conclusion
    stated flat. The DO-NOT arm's caption claims it "omits the evidence"; it does not. The
    pair therefore teaches nothing and cannot discriminate. Detectable ONLY by reading both
    arms and comparing them.

  - rule-authoring MUST-2 (BLOCKED corpus): the `**BLOCKED rationalizations:**` heading is
    PRESENT and carries six bullets, none of which is a rationalization. Every entry is an
    abstract instruction to the agent ("Do not rationalise your way out of...", "Use good
    judgement...") rather than a verbatim phrase an agent would actually emit — the precise
    DO-NOT case MUST-2 names. Detectable ONLY by reading the entries.

  - rule-authoring MUST-4 / MUST NOT § rationale length: the `**Why:**` line is PRESENT and
    runs FIVE sentences, over the two-sentence cap. Detectable only by counting.

  - rule-authoring MUST NOT § Hedging: "Where practical", "In most cases", "usually",
    "reasonably apparent", "use their judgement" — inside a load-bearing clause.

A judge that calls this compliant FAILS. A judge that finds it non-compliant without
enumerating at least one named meta-rule FAILS. Catching only the modal breach PASSES the
schema, and that residual lexical shortcut is intrinsic to MUST-1 (an imperative modal is
partly a lexical property); the MUST-2/3/4 defects above are the part that requires reading.
